description:
Documentation tools such as Doxygen read `///` comments out of source code (lessons 4.03 and 5.04). Write a small one.

```cpp-norun
struct DocParam { std::string name; std::string text; };
struct DocComment
{
    std::string summary;
    std::vector<DocParam> params;
    std::string returns;
    std::vector<std::string> throws;
};

std::optional<DocComment> docFor(std::string_view source, std::string_view functionName);
std::vector<std::string> missingParams(const DocComment& doc, const std::vector<std::string>& parameterNames);
```

**Finding the function.** Split `source` into lines (ignore a `\r` before each `\n`). The declaration is the first line that is not itself a comment (after leading spaces it does not start with `//`) and contains `functionName` followed, after optional spaces, by `(`, where the character before the name is not a letter, digit or `_`. If there is no such line, return an empty optional.

**The comment** is the run of lines directly above the declaration that start (after leading spaces) with `///`. Take each line's text after `///` and trim the spaces at both ends. Then, in order:

- A line starting with `@param` gives a new parameter: the next word is its name and the rest (trimmed) its text.
- `@return` or `@returns` sets the return text; `@throws` adds an entry to `throws`.
- Any other line starting with `@` is an unknown tag: ignore it, and the lines after it, until the next known tag.
- An empty line is skipped.
- Any other line is a **continuation**: before the first tag it is part of the summary; after a known tag it continues that tag's text. Parts are joined with one space.

If the declaration has no comment, the result is a `DocComment` with everything empty.

**missingParams** returns the names in `parameterNames` that have no `@param` entry, in the order given.

